57,960,961,964,965,189],{},"In Rust, ",[18,940,20],{}," is a ",[110,943,944],{},"pattern match",", not just a binding. The thing after ",[18,947,20],{}," is a pattern that the right-hand side is matched against — for simple ",[18,950,951],{},"let x = 5",", the pattern is just a variable binding (which always matches). But because it's a pattern, you can destructure tuples, arrays, slices, and structs ",[129,954,955],{},"at the moment of binding",", pulling values out into named variables in one expression. This is why you can write ",[18,958,959],{},"let (a, b) = pair;"," — ",[18,962,963],{},"pair"," is being matched against the pattern ",[18,966,967],{},"(a, b)",[106,969,970,971,973,974,977,978,980,981,984,985,988,989,992],{},"You reach for destructuring-",[18,972,20],{}," whenever a function returns a composite value (a tuple of results, a struct with fields you care about, a ",[18,975,976],{},"Result"," you want to unpack) and you want named access to its parts without a follow-up field-access line. For ",[18,979,20],{}," (and function parameters), the pattern must be ",[110,982,983],{},"irrefutable"," — it must match every possible value of the type, otherwise the compiler rejects it (use ",[18,986,987],{},"let ... else"," or ",[18,990,991],{},"match"," for refutable patterns).",[23,994,995],{"language":25},[27,996,998],{"className":29,"code":997,"language":25,"meta":31,"style":31},"let (a, b, c) = (1, 2, 3);\nlet [first, ..] = [1, 2, 3];        \u002F\u002F slice pattern (limited on stable)\nlet (x, ..) = (1, 2, 3, 4);         \u002F\u002F ignore rest\nlet Point { x, y } = point;          \u002F\u002F struct destructuring\nlet (Ok(v) | Err(v)) = result.map(|n| n + 1).map_err(|e| 0); \u002F\u002F or-pattern binding\n",[18,999,1000,1024,1058,1094,1112],{"__ignoreMap":31},[35,1001,1002,1004,1007,1009,1012,1014,1016,1018,1020,1022],{"class":37,"line":38},[35,1003,20],{"class":41},[35,1005,1006],{"class":44}," (a, b, c) ",[35,1008,48],{"class":41},[35,1010,1011],{"c